Surface the emitting aspect's identity on quirk entries.

A quirk entry is built with the emitting aspect's `identity` already on it
(nix/lib/aspects/fx/handlers/emit-classes.nix, `mkEntry`), but assembly drops
every field except `module` on the first line that touches it, so a collector
can never tell two emitters apart.

`pipe.withProvenance` partly covers this, but only at SCOPE granularity: local
emissions are all tagged with `currentScopeId`, so two aspects emitting into one
pipe at the same host scope are indistinguishable. That is the gap -- a
provider that mints one credential per consumer cannot do so when two aspects on
one host are separate consumers.

This preserves the entry's identity alongside the value, under `__emitter`, for
pipes that ask for it. The default path is unchanged: `extractValue` still
returns the bare module, so every existing collector sees exactly what it saw
before.

--- a/nix/lib/aspects/fx/assemble-pipes.nix
+++ b/nix/lib/aspects/fx/assemble-pipes.nix
@@ -12,7 +12,17 @@
   # Extract raw quirk value from a pipe entry.
   # Pipe entries are raw emit-class params with __isPipeEntry = true.
   # The actual quirk value is in the `module` field.
   extractValue = entry: entry.module or entry;
+
+  # The same, keeping the EMITTER's identity beside the value.
+  #
+  # `mkEntry` in handlers/emit-classes.nix already stamps each entry with the
+  # emitting aspect's `identity`; `extractValue` above discards it. A collector
+  # that mints one thing per emitter -- a credential per consumer, say -- needs
+  # finer granularity than `withProvenance`, whose tag is the emitting SCOPE and
+  # so cannot separate two aspects at one host.
+  extractValueWithEmitter =
+    entry:
+    if entry ? module && entry ? identity then
+      { __emitter = entry.identity; value = entry.module; }
+    else
+      entry;
